{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-357534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/d15f201b8f2a8521c68321b9ce15e5b13091fc899728822526d11cdcee08a393.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=2-\\frac{6}{x+1}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=5$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA=-\\,{\\int }_{1}^{a}(2-\\frac{6}{x+1})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{5}(2-\\frac{6}{x+1})\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-357534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=2-\\frac{6}{x+1}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{6}{x+1} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{6}{x+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{6}{x+1} & =2 \\\\ 6 & =2(x+1) \\\\ 6 & =2x+2 \\\\ 4 & =2x \\\\ 2 & =x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=1$ and $x=5$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [1,5],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=5.$\n\nSo, $a=2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=\\,-\\,{\\int }_{1}^{2}(2-\\frac{6}{x+1})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{5}(2-\\frac{6}{x+1})\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-357534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-357534" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-357534" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-73894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/0e0466f463644e25cd2a615c445b6b32a6d9ae236c44658609cd778c5222fcae.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-4$ to $x=-1$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-4}^{a}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{-1}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-73894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y={x}^{2}+3x.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+3x \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+3x \\\\ x(x+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=0$ or $x=-3.$ The only solution between $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ is $x=-3.$\n\nSince $-3\\in [-4,-1]$, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-4$ to $x=-3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=-1.$\n\nSo, $a=-3$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-4}^{-3}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}({x}^{2}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-73894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-73894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-73894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2.5"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{9}{4}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "-\\sqrt{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-73894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-357547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/196dcb953e409bd1688e77661e4a635aa92b7edb837f26bb2666592b3ff54a90.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=2-\\frac{8}{x-1}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=9$ is given by the following integral expression below: \n\n$$\nA=-\\,{\\int }_{3}^{a}(2-\\frac{8}{x-1})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{a}^{9}(2-\\frac{8}{x-1})\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-357547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=2-\\frac{8}{x-1}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{8}{x-1} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{x-1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{x-1} & =2 \\\\ 8 & =2(x-1) \\\\ 8 & =2x-2 \\\\ 10 & =2x \\\\ 5 & =x \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=3$ and $x=9$ is $x=5.$\n\nSince $5\\in [3,9],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=5.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=5$ to $x=9.$\n\nSo, $a=5$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=-\\,{\\int }_{3}^{5}(2-\\frac{8}{x-1})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{5}^{9}(2-\\frac{8}{x-1})\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-357547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-357547" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-357547" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/8b729d243b344df6b26757773bf0521dea80da34fc727541d24babd3732563cf.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{a}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{5/2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4} & =-4 \\\\ {x}^{4} & =16 \\\\ \\sqrt[4]{{x}^{4}} & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt[4]{{2}^{4}} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution between $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is $x=2.$\n\nSince $2\\in [-1,\\frac{5}{2}],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nSo, $a=2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{5/2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-140583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140583" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/72ecb00845c906fd44da9b5f559fb06445b3d8ac154064743fd433cc895e1916.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x-2}+1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=0$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{a}(\\frac{4}{x-2}+1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{0}(\\frac{4}{x-2}+1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{x-2}+1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{x-2}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{x-2} & =-1 \\\\ 4 & =-(x-2) \\\\ 4 & =-x+2 \\\\ 2 & =-x \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-2\\in [-5,0],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-5$ to $x=-2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\nSo, $a=-2$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-5}^{-2}(\\frac{4}{x-2}+1)\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(\\frac{4}{x-2}+1)\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-140581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140581" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-74159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/cd6555800f4b7c7a7ae76ef9dd75ebca543ea4898b4205dd24df15f52f95653d.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y=-4{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-74159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\in [-1,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis.  Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=1+1=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-74159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-74159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-74159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-74159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/14bd6415062b5e08f183e22059cffa2893442c87c391dfdb1c020c942e15af83.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0\\in [-1,1]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-1,0]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,1],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54475" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-54475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54475" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-345648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+6x-5$ and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=7.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-345648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $5\\in [2,7],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=5.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=5$ to $x=7.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/e14a891e924a6911a0df962dc5c5b41c90b0c411de4ebe1f7e52ec1953e4124c.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [5,7]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{5}^{7}(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative.\n\nTherefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{2}^{5}x\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{5}+3{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{5}-5x\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{5}^{3}-{2}^{3}]+3[{5}^{2}-{2}^{2}]-5[5-2] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(125-8)+3(25-4)-5(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{117}{3}+63-15 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [5,7],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{5}^{7}(-{x}^{2}+6x-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-6{\\int }_{5}^{7}x\\,\\text{d}x+5{\\int }_{5}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{5}^{7}-3{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{5}^{7}+5x\\,{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{7}^{3}-{5}^{3}]-3[{7}^{2}-{5}^{2}]+5[7-5] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(343-125)-3(49-25)+5(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{218}{3}-72+10 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =9+\\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-345648" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-345648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-345648" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{59}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $3\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/219d7aa5b976c97d47a96d0c94c3d61d139d5ddddc55b82d0df68f19ad6d5149.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [3,4]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[{3}^{2}-{1}^{2}]-\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[9-1]-\\frac{1}{3}[27-1] \\\\  & =12-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [3,4],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{4}(3x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{3}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{3}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{3}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[{4}^{2}-{3}^{2}]+\\frac{1}{3}[{4}^{3}-{3}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}[16-9]+\\frac{1}{3}[64-27] \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}+\\frac{37}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{10}{3}+\\frac{11}{6}=\\frac{31}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{31}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140607" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{31}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/2ffa9585853d1230c9664a27dffcd832aec5cdff3fa1d3f01c9012d2835d101e.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3,$ as shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\in [0,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=2$ to $x=3.$\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [0,2]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[{2}^{3}-{0}^{3}]+[{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[8-0]+4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [2,3],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{2}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{3}^{3}-{2}^{3}]-[{3}^{2}-{2}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[27-8]-[9-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140603" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-140603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140603" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=9-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $3\\in [-3,6]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/70df9534ea4f69993871888a5f751ecd6aea9d1e0aaaf128d55442d9913b3dd5.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-3,3]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9x{|}_{-3}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =[9(3)-9(-3)]-[\\frac{(3)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =54-18 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [3,6]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{6}(9-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-9x{|}_{3}^{6}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =-9[6-3]+[\\frac{(6)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =-27+63 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=36+36=72.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-117444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "18"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "54"] ["math" "72"] ["math" "90"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "72"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=27-3{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =27 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $3\\in [-3,5]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=3$ to $x=5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/6b3ca7be487585b296aaeb0b3fa7eb4ce8b9b4f9ad35d52baefa04f2fbbed621.png)\n\nFor $x\\in [-3,3]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27x{|}_{-3}^{3}-{x}^{3}{|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =27[3-(-3)]-[(3)^{3}-(-3)^{3}] \\\\  & =162-54 \\\\  & =108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [3,5]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{3}^{5}(27-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{3}^{5}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-27x{|}_{3}^{5}+{x}^{3}{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =-27[5-3]+[(5)^{3}-(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =-54+98 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=108+44=152.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "104"] ["math" "152"] ["math" "200"] ["math" "44"] ["math" "98"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-117447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "152"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/7ffd75051b71a3b8665ac68418ff24dac2124a6b7ff50633a0a5843df9cff196.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{5}-x$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{4}-1) & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1) & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 1$ or $x=0.$ \nThis gives us two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-54522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{2}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-54522" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}-({x}^{5}-x)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/a4871287696f20009bd7d2b8793965c9f68e35faa4ad150477643f1d3ec77e38.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y={x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({x}^{2}-x-6) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(x+2)(x-3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=-2,x=0$ or $x=3.$ Since $0\\in [-2,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nFor $x\\in [-2,0]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-2}^{3}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140577" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-({x}^{5}-{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/2a32d95d98a2c2cefe08b492c368a99df61a8cc3a1f66018a6dfe444e65146b8.png)\n\nWhich integral expression gives the total area enclosed between the curve $y=9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the limits of integration. We find the $x$-intercepts of the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(9-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}(3-x)(3+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $x=\\pm 3$ or $x=0.$ Since $0\\in [-3,3],$ we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-3,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}=-{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [0,3]$, we have\n\n$$\n{A}_{2}={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}={\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "19c09806423cb68bfe0c6d5e5490bef5df74535818589a8fd9de573d9d03ca08"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-3}^{3}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-9}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{9}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-9}^{9}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "q-140580" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-3}^{0}-(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(9{x}^{3}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/state.json" "file_sha256" "c839c35110c557329d92461379a78c9c503908f65bdb4ee7d424f17dc099ae8f"} {"question" "e-3888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=x^3-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=2$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{a} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{a}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/57d01ea60e3cd9770f2ef978e46e2cef7b69a9fcdc2ac478d5b6c73d8f366c7b.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3888.json" "file_sha256" "672a7a749017fbb69c0ce2f425e85fadf8bc58b978edb004b766c1a59329aebb"} {"question" "e-3888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=x^3-1.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{3}-1 \\\\ (0) & ={x}^{3}-1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1 \\in [-2,2]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x={\\color{blue}{1}}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$ to $x=2.$\n\nSo, $a={\\color{blue}1}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{\\color{blue}1} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{\\color{blue}1}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3888.json" "file_sha256" "672a7a749017fbb69c0ce2f425e85fadf8bc58b978edb004b766c1a59329aebb"} {"question" "e-3887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^3$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/0d3ede3cfbec765228ea184c74e0cb35f1df2a9340bd1cb703a0990249fecf30.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3887.json" "file_sha256" "61cd36357b8a1b03cb152cd5acd36868b16c11f7caf656be6d354269a5dd8220"} {"question" "e-3887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0 \\in \\left[-1,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in  [-1,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-1}^{0}  \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\nA_1 = -\\int_{-1}^{0}  \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\nFor $x \\in\\left[0,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]$, we have\n\n$$\nA_2 = \\int_{0}^{3/2}\\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 = - \\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{3/2} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}  \\:\\textrm{d}x .\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{-1}^{0}+{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{8}]}_{0}^{3/2} \\\\  & =-[0-\\frac{1}{8}]+[\\frac{81}{128}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{81}{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{97}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3887.json" "file_sha256" "61cd36357b8a1b03cb152cd5acd36868b16c11f7caf656be6d354269a5dd8220"} {"question" "e-3889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=x^3-9x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3889.json" "file_sha256" "b943aa10fa2aab6ef3ac30b7fe4a86228cf87a761f34948d461f527af937e133"} {"question" "e-3889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-9x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ x(x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0,$ $x=-3,$ and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $0 \\in [-3,3]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/assets/29a51c9892de59037e84bace2f8b5822222c3056a05af861cbd741390a437a61.png)\n\nFor $x \\in [-3,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-9{\\int }_{-3}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-3}^{0}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-3}^{0} \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{(-3)^{4}}{4}]-9[0-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(x^3-9x\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+9{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0]+9[\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0] \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 =  \\dfrac{81}{4} + \\dfrac{81}{4} = \\dfrac{81}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013598/example-3889.json" "file_sha256" "b943aa10fa2aab6ef3ac30b7fe4a86228cf87a761f34948d461f527af937e133"}] "usage" {"e-3889" [] "e-3887" [] "e-3888" [] "q-54475" []} "basis" 1169 "retractions" [[17592186111247 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/q-54475.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region between the curve $y = \\frac{1}{2}x^{3}$ and the $x$-axis from $x =-1$ to $x = 1$, as shown above."] [17592186110406 :question/problem "The total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=x^3-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=2$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a$?\n$A = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{a} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{a}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/57a34212c16bef10ef454f07479d2053.png)"] [17592186110406 :question/worked-solution "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=x^3-1$. So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= x^{3} - 1 \\\\\n(0) &= x^{3} - 1 \\\\\nx^{3} &= 1 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1 \\in [-2,2]$, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x={\\color{blue}{1}}$ to $x=2$.\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\nand the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA = \\displaystyle -\\int_{-2}^{\\color{blue}1} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{\\color{blue}1}^{2} (x^3-1) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$"] [17592186110405 :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x^3$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/ba25ac7d1486034bfea03158e8407dae.png)"] [17592186110405 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}x^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n0 \\in \\left[-1,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\n, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$.\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [-1,0]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\nA_1 = -\\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor\n\n$$\nx \\in\\left[0,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\n, we have\n\n$$\nA_2 = \\int_{0}^{3/2}\\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThe total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 =-\\int_{-1}^{0} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{3/2} \\dfrac{x^3}{2} \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFinally, we carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= - ∫_{-1}^{0}\\frac{x^{3}}{2}dx + ∫_{0}^{3/2}\\frac{x^{3}}{2}dx \\\\\n&= - [\\frac{x^{4}}{8}]_{-1}^{0} + [\\frac{x^{4}}{8}]_{0}^{3/2} \\\\\n&= - [0 - \\frac{1}{8}] + [\\frac{81}{128} - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} + \\frac{81}{128} \\\\\n&= \\frac{97}{128}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186110407 :question/problem "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y=x^3-9x$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=3$."] [17592186110407 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - 9x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 9) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 3)(x + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$, $x=-3$, and $x=3$ are our real solutions. Since $0 \\in [-3,3]$, we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-3$ to $x=0$.\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1432/Source/Images/2a563bcdf5a67c69214ca4fcde498ea4.png)\n\nFor $x \\in [-3,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{1} &= ∫_{a}^{b}y(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-3}^{0}(x^{3} - 9x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-3}^{0}x^{3}dx - 9∫_{-3}^{0}xdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{4}}{4} \\mid _{-3}^{0} - \\frac{9x^{2}}{2} \\mid _{-3}^{0} \\\\\n&= [0 - ((-3)^{4})/(4)] - 9[0 - ((-3)^{2})/(2)] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{81}{4} + \\frac{81}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{81}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for $x \\in [0,3]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} y(x) \\:\\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{3} \\left(x^3-9x\\right) \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{2} &= - ∫_{0}^{3}(x^{3} - 9x)dx \\\\\n&= - ∫_{0}^{3}x^{3}dx + 9∫_{0}^{3}xdx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{x^{4}}{4} \\mid _{0}^{3} + \\frac{9x^{2}}{2} \\mid _{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= - [((3)^{4})/(4) - 0] + 9[((3)^{2})/(2) - 0] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{81}{4} + \\frac{81}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{81}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA = A_1+A_2 = \\dfrac{81}{4} + \\dfrac{81}{4} = \\dfrac{81}{2}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186109578 17592186109579 17592186109580 17592186109581 17592186109582] "immutable_fields" [[17592186109577 :answer-field/correct] [17592186109577 :answer-field/id] [17592186109577 :answer-field/key] [17592186109577 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
